# SENMA Git rules

## No automatic Git history changes

Never automatically:

- commit;
- push;
- amend;
- rebase;
- merge;
- reset;
- force-push;

unless the user explicitly authorizes that exact action.

## Staging

Never use:

```bash
git add .
```

Never use broad staging when the working tree may contain unrelated changes.

Use explicit file paths:

```bash
git add path/to/file1 path/to/file2
```

Before committing, always inspect:

```bash
git status --short
git diff --cached --check
git diff --cached --name-status
git diff --cached
```

## Dirty working trees

If unrelated files are modified:

- do not edit them;
- do not stage them;
- do not revert them;
- do not include them in the task commit.

Work around existing unrelated changes safely.

## Commit authorization

When a task checkpoint is complete:

- propose a commit strategy;
- list exact files per commit;
- propose commit messages;
- wait for explicit authorization.

Do not interpret task approval as commit authorization unless the user explicitly asks to create the commit.

## Commit quality

Prefer thematic commits.

A commit should represent one coherent concern, for example:

```text
fix(...)
feat(...)
test(...)
docs(...)
```

Avoid mixing:
- production implementation;
- unrelated tests;
- unrelated documentation;
- opportunistic cleanup.

## After commit

Verify:

```bash
git status --short
git log -1 --oneline
```

For multiple commits:

```bash
git log -N --oneline
```

Do not push unless explicitly requested.
